Where the power is dissipated depends on the resistances and the
connection. If it is a series connection (the same current through
each resistance) then yes, more power is dissipated in the higher
resistance. However, with a parallel connection (the same voltage
across each resistance) more power is dissipated in the lower
resistance. I would say this would be a parallel connection (similiar
to a lightning rod and grounding wire). The ratio of the powers that
are dissipated is the inverse of the ratio of the resistances for a
parallel connection. IAAEE (I Am An Electrical Engineer).

I like this idea. It actually gives the chains a use other than
feeding to metallivore pets.

